<section class="section section--tint" aria-labelledby="act-title">
  <div class="wrap">
    <header class="section-head">
      <h2 id="act-title">Reported activity, and what it means for your accounts</h2>
      <p>These reports describe some units and not all of them. The accounting points are our general analysis. Check the treatment of your own goods and processes.</p>
    </header>
    <section class="data-table-wrap" tabindex="0" aria-label="Reported activity and accounting points, scrolls sideways on small screens">
      <table class="data-table">
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">Area</th><th scope="col">Reported activity</th><th scope="col">Accounting points to raise</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><th scope="row">Okhla</th><td>Garment and printing units are mentioned, along with flatted factories</td><td>Job work and export paperwork for garments. Paper stock and work-in-progress by order for printers</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Wazirpur</th><td>Metal-related units, with pickling mentioned in an environmental survey</td><td>Material yield and scrap, process charges, and any compliance costs</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Mayapuri</th><td>Described as a small-industry area and a metal and scrap market</td><td>Scrap purchases from unregistered sellers, weight-based stock, and ask about the GST treatment of scrap</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Naraina</th><td>Heavy kitchen and hotel equipment mentioned in one report</td><td>Made-to-order projects, customer advances and installation</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Bawana and Narela</th><td>Plots and flatted units under government schemes. Reported auctions in 2024 and freehold policy talk in 2025</td><td>Lease, allotment and premium records, and whether costs are capital or revenue</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Narela</th><td>Plastics, cable, food processing and footwear named in a 2022 university study, with repeated press reports of fires at slipper and shoe units</td><td>For footwear: GST set by price per pair, credit building up when materials carry a higher rate, and stock in pairs and sizes</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Patparganj</th><td>Offset printing described in a 2022 trade report, and garment units reported to have shrunk</td><td>As for Okhla: paper stock and work in progress for printers, and job work for garments</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Mangolpuri</th><td>Press reports name a shoe factory, a circuit-breaker unit and a rubber unit, so no single trade is shown</td><td>Follow your own product's workflow. Footwear points are on the Narela page</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </section>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section" aria-labelledby="common-title">
  <div class="wrap two-col">
    <div>
      <h2 id="common-title">What most units share</h2>
      <ul class="tick-list plain-list">
        <li>Raw material, work in progress and finished goods records</li>
        <li>Job work challans, and returns on time</li>
        <li>Wages, overtime, and PF and ESI where applicable</li>
        <li>A fixed asset register for machines</li>
        <li>TDS on contractors and rent, where applicable</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="para">The full workflow is on {% routeLink "/industries/accounting-for-manufacturers/", "accounting for manufacturers" %}.</p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h2>Choosing someone</h2>
      <p>Ask whether they have handled a process like yours, and if they will visit to see how material moves and to observe a stock count. Those two things are hard to do from a distance, and are where location can genuinely matter.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

